WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:01.350 align:start position:0%
 
i mean what an extraordinary week it's  been um 40 of conservative mps voting to  say they've had no confidence in their  prime minister  do you think kirsten missed a bit of a  trick at prime minister's questions and  not really pushing that  no i thought that kiyosama at prime  minister's questions today actually  pushed the reality that people are  facing in the nhs in england unable to  get a gp  appointment the weights at a e  the cancer waiting lists that have been  going up over the past 12 years and  that's the reality people are facing  and we have a government now that isn't  dealing with those issues what we've  seen this week is a government in  absolute chaos we have 148 conservative  mps  who agree with us there's an opposition  that they've no confidence whatsoever in  the prime minister then you've got 211  conservative mps who are saying that the  prime minister's law breaking the prime  minister's contempt for people's  sacrifices during the pandemic is an  acceptable thing that they can support  what you now have is a divided  conservative party that isn't going to  be able to tackle the challenges of the  country  do you think though i mean boris johnson  argues that look  he he's got the vote out of the way he's  drawn a line under it he's got a  majority and actually you know even if  you speak to the most fierce critics  like tim lowton who just had on the  program they seem to accept the result  and do you think that he's now going to  lead the conservatives into the next  election  uh whether the prime minister leads the  conservatives into the next election uh  or not we will be making the case that  we've had 12 years of conservative  failure and looking at it from the  opposition benches this week let's not  hide from what's happened here a  majority of the conservative mps has  voted to support this prime minister  this is a prime minister whose focus is  on saving himself this is a government  we saw a document conservative mps had  put around saying that the purpose of  this government is actually to keep the  prime minister in office they are not  focused on the problem i mean to be fair  they would say that you know in just the  last few weeks they have they have  launched a bumper package to help with  the cost of living going further than  the labour party would have done uh on  uh with the windfall tax and on energy  bills so  it's not right to say that they're not  doing anything is it the conservative  party has been in power for 12 years is  tired and out of ideas it's labor that's  made the political weather it's the  windfall tax proposal that we actually  pushed for months and months and if by  the way they'd adopted it five months  ago nearly six months ago when we first  spoke about it people would have been in  a much better position now they are out  of ideas and this is a tired government  now on the 21st of june we're expecting  strikes on the trains or on the  underground network the rmt say that  it's going to be the biggest outbreak of  industrial action in the uk since 1989.  do you back the strikes  what i would like to see is a resolution  to this nobody ever wants to see strikes  nobody wants to see workers in a  position of making those sacrifices  nobody wants to see the disruption that  this is going to cause to the public and  what we need to come in there because i  mean no one would disagree that you want  to see a resolution the question is you  know where do you get that resolution if  you can't reach one would you like  strike so do you back strikes under the  current situation what i want to see is  more dialogue we've seen the three dates  that the rmt has spoken about there is  still absolutely time for dialogue that  dialogue has to take place and we can't  have either government ministers somehow  washing their hands of responsibility  about this so more dialogue does that  mean that the rmt should pause the  strikes in favor of more dialogue  because is that what you're saying but  there is still time available we're not  right up against at the moment the date  that the rmt is saying i think it's the  21st of june is the first date that  they're talking about there is time for  dialogue we need to get all the parties  around the table there needs to be the  government by the way not distracted  permanently as they are by their own  internal problems who actually take some  responsibility for facilitating these  talks and preventing those strikes from  happening i spoke to the rmt's mick  lynch on the sunday program a couple  weeks ago  he said i've seen no response from the  labour front bench that says we support  the workers in their struggles if we  just get this bland democratic party  sitting in the center of politics taking  their instructions off the daily mail to  some extent and not actually getting  behind workers struggles you have to ask  yourself why do they actually call  themselves the labour party well i don't  agree with that  interpretation we are on the side of  working people up and down the country  whether they're working in the rail  industry whether  they are in the wider public working in  many other industries what we need to  see is an actual cost of living package  we need an emergency budget we shouldn't  be having the national insurance  increase can you see why mitt lynch  thinks that the labour party is a bit  bland if you just say oh yeah we want  more dialogue but it feels like you're  not really giving a definitive answer on  what you feel about the strikes  no i i fundamentally disagree because i  think strike action is always difficult  and dialogue is the only way that you  find a solution so that's why i'm  merging that dialogue in those  particular circumstances but i don't  accept at all that what we said about  the cost of living is somehow bland it's  our campaigning on the windfall tax  that's caused the government to actually  have to adopt our policy we are the ones  calling for an emergency budget saying  there shouldn't be the national  insurance increase we're the ones who  are talking about investing a climate  investment pledge 28 billion pounds uh  each year insulating homes up and down  the country we are the party that is  putting those ideas forward do you  support the government's package on the  cost of living  uh  we've we've been very clear on things  like where the chancellor actually  reduced fuel duty that of course those  are things that we would support but the  idea that what the chancellor has put  forward is sufficient for everybody out  there is actually facing i'm afraid it's  for the birds it isn't and what the  chancellor has had to do already was to  come back when we pushed him to do so he  should come back again with an emergency  budget but with what in the the  emergency budget what specifically would  you like to see right well first of all  let's see the reversal of the national  insurance increase  let's see the chancellor of the  exchequer take away vat on energy for  example is another thing that the  chancellor could do and let's see the  chancellor as well  for once actually have a long-term plan  for tackling growth in the in the 10  years before the pandemic the average  growth per year was running at below 2  percent there needs to be a strategy for  growth in my area of international trade  by the way we see the government rushing  to complete  initial trade deals what they're not  doing and i see it because i'm out there  visiting exporters on the front line  they're not providing the support to  businesses and to exporters that they  need to actually be able to make those  trade deals work for communities up and  down the country okay thank you very  much for being on the show today uh nick  at thomas simmons there

